Part I — Formal Definition of LangOne
LangOne is defined below as a pure mathematical object. The definition is complete in the sense that every structural property of the language follows from these components. There are no informal supplements, no conventions, no traditions. The language is entirely contained in the 6-tuple that follows.
1. The Language as a 6-Tuple
L  =  (M, G, Ω, ∂, ⊛, τ)

M   —  a separable Riemannian manifold, dim = ℵ₀ (countably infinite)
G   —  the metric tensor field over M; encodes semantic distance
Ω   —  the family of admissible field configurations Φ : M → ℝⁿ
∂   —  the boundary operator; defines null manifold constraints
⊛   —  the resonance coupling operator (non-commutative, non-associative)
τ   —  the layer functor mapping Ω across the five-level architecture
L in full: LangOne is not a set of symbols. It is a 6-tuple. A geometric space, a measure of distance, the valid states, the forbidden states, how states interact, and how meaning scales across layers. There are no tokens. There is no alphabet. The language IS the geometry.
Each component of the tuple is irreducible. Remove any one and the result is not a degraded LangOne — it is a different object entirely. The metric tensor G without the manifold M is undefined. The resonance operator ⊛ without the admissible family Ω has no domain. The layer functor τ without the boundary operator ∂ produces fields that violate well-formedness. All six components are necessary and together sufficient.
2. The Five Axioms
These five axioms are the constitutive constraints of LangOne. They are not guidelines or best practices. A structure that violates any axiom is, by definition, not LangOne. They are listed in order of ontological priority — A1 is presupposed by A2, A2 by A3, and so on.
Axiom 1 — Continuity
∀ Φ ∈ Ω :  Φ ∈ C∞(M, ℝⁿ)
∄ discrete Φ ∈ Ω
Every valid LangOne state is infinitely differentiable. Discrete states — symbols, tokens, integers, binary flags — do not exist in Ω. This is not an approximation rule; it is an axiom. Violation = not LangOne.
Axiom 2 — Field Primacy
meaning(Φ)  :=  geometry(Φ, M, G)
meaning(Φ)  ≠  f(symbols)
meaning(Φ)  ≠  f(syntax)
Meaning is a geometric property of a field configuration in relation to the manifold, not a property assigned by convention to a symbol. No dictionary. No interpretation. Meaning is shape.
Axiom 3 — Uncertainty-Intrinsic
∀ Φ ∈ Ω :  uncertainty(Φ, x)  :=  κ⁻¹(Φ, x)

where κ is the local curvature radius of Φ at x.
  Sharp attractor  →  high κ  →  low uncertainty
  Flat plateau     →  low κ  →  high uncertainty
Uncertainty is not an annotation. It is the local curvature of the field. You cannot mark a state as uncertain — it either has a sharp curvature profile or it does not. Every state is epistemically honest by construction.
Axiom 4 — Non-Sequentiality
∄ canonical ordering on Ω
composition  :=  superposition, not concatenation

Φ1 ⊕ Φ2  =  Φ2 ⊕ Φ1       (superposition: commutative)
Φ1 ⊛ Φ2  ≠  Φ2 ⊛ Φ1       (coupling: non-commutative)
LangOne has no sentence order. Superposition is commutative. But resonance coupling is not — the way two fields interact depends on which is the source and which is the receiver. There is directionality without sequentiality.
Axiom 5 — Emergence
∃ Φ_collective ∈ Attr(D_multi)  such that
  Φ_collective ∉ ⋃ᵢ Attr(Dᵢ)

i.e. collective attractors exist that no individual
system can reach through single-system dynamics alone.
The axiom of emergence is a structural guarantee, not a tendency. The multi-system dynamics have attractors that are geometrically inaccessible from any single-system trajectory. Intelligence that cannot be had alone can be had together.
