4. The Four Composition Operators
All LangOne expressions are composed from the six primitives using these four operators. The operators are ordered by structural complexity: superposition is purely additive, resonance coupling introduces nonlinear interaction, attractor binding finds geometric midpoints, and tensor bonding creates cross-domain structures.
Superposition  ⊕
(Φ1 ⊕ Φ2)(x)  :=  Φ1(x) + Φ2(x)

Properties: commutative, associative, linear
Both fields coexist at every point simultaneously. Not "A then B" — "A and B at once, everywhere." The meaning is the vector sum of both configurations.
Resonance Coupling  ⊛
(Φ1 ⊛ Φ2)(x)  :=  Φ1(x)·Φ2(x) + R(Φ1, Φ2, x)

Properties: non-commutative, non-associative
Φ1⊛Φ2 ≠ Φ2⊛Φ1  in general
Multiplicative interaction plus a resonance term R capturing nonlinear interference. Source and receiver are not interchangeable. New meaning emerges that neither field contained alone. This is the operator of cognition.
Attractor Binding  ⊞
Φ1 ⊞ Φ2  :=  argmin_Φ [d_G(Φ,Φ1) + d_G(Φ,Φ2)]

Properties: commutative, geodesically defined
Finds the geodesic midpoint between two attractor basins in the manifold's metric. Not a compromise — the exact geometric center. The answer to "what do these two things have in common" is this operator applied to their fields.
Tensor Bonding  ⊗
Φ1 ⊗ Φ2  ∈  Ω(M₁ × M₂)

Properties: bilinear, creates object on product manifold
Outer product across domain boundaries. Creates a joint field over M₁×M₂. Neither field is changed. A new object is created that lives in both domains simultaneously. Cross-domain knowledge, analogy, and the unity of science are all tensor bonds.
