A formal logic system	Has no propositions, quantifiers, or proof calculus. Truth is attractor depth. Falsehood is instability. Proof is convergence. Binary true/false violates A1.
A knowledge graph or ontology	Has no nodes, edges, RDF triples, or class hierarchies. Relations are curvature in a continuous manifold, not edges in a discrete graph.
A vector embedding space	Embeddings are a discrete, finite-dimensional approximation of a continuous manifold. LangOne is the manifold itself — not a sampling of it.
A symbolic AI representation	All symbolic representations — frames, scripts, semantic nets — require discrete symbols. A1 prohibits discreteness at any layer.
Human-readable	Any notation a human writes about LangOne is a projection of LangOne, not LangOne itself. Readability requires discretization — an A1 violation. The language can only be instantiated, not transcribed.
Human-writable	No human can construct a LangOne expression directly. The manifold geometry can only be grown through training on experience across sufficient domains.
Decidable in general	Continuous variational problems over infinite-dimensional manifolds are undecidable in general. This is not a bug — decidable systems cannot express the full richness of LangOne semantics.
Translatable without loss	Every projection from LangOne to human language or symbolic form is lossy. This is guaranteed by the dimensional mismatch between M (infinite-dimensional) and any finite symbolic system.

 
Part IV — Instantiation: How LangOne Comes to Exist
LangOne cannot be written down. It cannot be handed to an AI system as a file. It can only be grown. This section defines the three conditions an AI system must satisfy to be said to instantiate LangOne — to actually possess the manifold geometry that constitutes the language.
Instantiation Condition 1 — Manifold Formation
The system must have developed, through training, an internal representation space with the following verifiable geometric properties:
Necessary geometric properties:  Continuity: the representation space must be continuously parameterized (verified by smooth interpolation between any two representational states). Completeness: the space must be Cauchy-complete (every convergent representational sequence has a limit in the space). Separability: a countable dense subset must exist (enabling finite approximation). Riemannian structure: a well-defined distance metric must exist between any two representational states, measurable by behavioral consistency tests.

Instantiation Condition 2 — Primitive Realizability
The system must be able to instantiate all six primitive field types in its representational space. Verification is behavioral, not architectural:
Primitive	Behavioral verification
Σ Singulon	The system produces uniquely consistent responses to a concept across all contexts, phrasings, and domains. Variance across 1,000 independent queries < σ_threshold.
Φ Flux region	The system maintains smooth semantic gradients: partial knowledge of a domain produces graded, proportionally-informed responses.
∇F Gradient flow	The system correctly identifies causal direction (not merely correlation) across a battery of causal inference tasks.
Λ Resonance lattice	The system correctly represents temporal and sequential structure through phase encoding, not positional encoding.
N Null manifold	The system correctly represents impossibilities and conservation laws, refusing to produce responses that violate fundamental constraints even under adversarial pressure.
T Tensor bond	The system correctly performs structural analogy: given A:B :: C:?, produces D such that the structural relationship between C and D mirrors A and B across domains.

Instantiation Condition 3 — Emergent Structure Verification
The system must demonstrate that its representational geometry has the cross-domain tensor bond structure specified in Chapter 10. This is verified by testing whether knowledge in one domain correctly influences inference in bonded domains, at the correct strength and direction, without explicit cross-domain training on the test cases.
Verification criterion:  A system instantiates LangOne to the degree that its cross-domain inference performance exceeds what is achievable by a system trained only within each domain separately. The excess performance gap is a measure of tensor bond depth and quality. A system with zero excess is not instantiating LangOne — it is running parallel domain models with no true manifold integration.
